# XSH sigaction()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.sigaction.001
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With act null, sigaction() stores the current action in *oact
           and changes nothing: the handler, the sa_mask (SIGUSR2) and
           the flag (SA_RESTART) installed before are reported, and the
           handler still runs when the signal is raised.
test       t/001-oact.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.002
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       sigaction() with both act and oact stores the previous action (a
           first handler) in *oact and installs the new one (a second
           handler), which then runs.
test       t/002-oact-and-act.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.003
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       SIGKILL and SIGSTOP in sa_mask are silently left out: sigaction()
           succeeds, and while the handler runs neither is in the
           signal mask, while another signal from sa_mask (SIGUSR2) is.
test       t/003-kill-stop-not-masked.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.004
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       Without SA_SIGINFO the function in sa_handler is called, with the
           signal number (SIGUSR1) as its argument.
test       t/004-one-argument.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.005
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With SA_SIGINFO (on SIGRTMIN) the function in sa_sigaction is
           called with the signal number and a siginfo_t: for kill() from
           the process itself, si_signo is the signal, si_code is SI_USER, and
           si_pid and si_uid are the sender's process ID and real user
           ID.
note       si_pid and si_uid for SI_USER come from XSH 2.4.3 and
           <signal.h>.
test       t/005-siginfo.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.006
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With SA_SIGINFO, a signal (SIGRTMIN) sent with sigqueue() reaches the handler
           with si_code SI_QUEUE and the value given (si_value.sival_int
           1234).
test       t/006-sigqueue-value.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.007
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With SIGCHLD caught and SA_NOCLDSTOP clear, SIGCHLD is generated
           when a child stops (accepted with sigtimedwait() within 5 s).
note       SIGCHLD has been mandatory since 2001 (standard issue SI-25).
test       t/007-nocldstop-clear.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.008
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With SIGCHLD caught and SA_NOCLDSTOP set, no SIGCHLD is generated
           when a child stops: after waitid(WSTOPPED|WNOWAIT) shows the
           stop, SIGCHLD is not pending.
test       t/008-nocldstop-set.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.009
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       XSI: with an alternate stack set by sigaltstack(), a handler
           installed with SA_ONSTACK runs on it (a local variable's
           address is inside it), and one installed without SA_ONSTACK
           does not.
test       t/009-onstack.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.010
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With SA_RESETHAND, the action for the signal (SIGRTMIN, with
           SA_SIGINFO) is SIG_DFL when the handler is entered and after it
           returns.
test       t/010-resethand.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.011
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With SIGCHLD caught and SA_NOCLDSTOP set, a child's termination
           still generates SIGCHLD (accepted with sigtimedwait() within
           5 s): the flag only suppresses the SIGCHLD for stops.
test       t/011-nocldstop-exit.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.012
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With SA_RESTART on a caught signal, read() on an empty pipe that
           the signal interrupts does not fail with EINTR: it restarts and
           returns the byte written later.
test       t/012-restart-set.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.013
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       Without SA_RESTART on a caught signal, read() on an empty pipe that
           the signal interrupts fails with EINTR.
test       t/013-restart-clear.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.014
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       While a handler installed without SA_NODEFER runs, the signal
           itself and the signals in sa_mask (SIGUSR2) are blocked, in
           addition to the mask before; when it returns, the earlier mask
           (SIGTERM blocked, SIGUSR1 and SIGUSR2 not) is back.
test       t/014-mask-in-handler.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.015
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With SA_NODEFER the signal is not added to the mask while its
           handler runs; if sa_mask includes it, it is blocked.
test       t/015-nodefer.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.016
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       Without SA_RESETHAND, a handler stays installed after it runs:
           raising the signal three times runs it three times.
test       t/016-stays-installed.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.017
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       After signal() installs a handler, passing the action reported by
           sigaction() back to sigaction() (after another handler was
           installed) makes the signal handled as signal() had set it up:
           the first handler runs.
note       The fields reported for a signal() action are unspecified;
           only the round trip is required.
test       t/017-signal-roundtrip.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.018
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      1988
says       sigaction() fails with EINVAL, returning -1, for a handler or
           SIG_IGN on SIGKILL and on SIGSTOP; the action stays SIG_DFL.
test       t/018-einval-uncatchable.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.019
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      1988
says       sigaction() fails with EINVAL for signal number 0, for -1 and for
           a number above every valid one (100000), with and without act.
test       t/019-einval-signo.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.020
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With SA_SIGINFO set for SIGRTMIN, three occurrences sent with
           sigqueue() while it is blocked are all delivered when it is
           unblocked, in the order sent (values 1, 2, 3).
nomutant   queuing is done by the kernel
test       t/020-queued-rt.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.021
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      2008
says       With SA_SIGINFO set for SIGUSR1 (not a realtime signal), three
           occurrences sent with sigqueue() while it is blocked are all
           delivered when it is unblocked, in the order sent.
note       Disputed (standard issue SI-24): this page, XSH 2.4.2 and
           sigqueue() require queuing for any signal with SA_SIGINFO,
           but <signal.h> supports the realtime behaviour only in
           SIGRTMIN-SIGRTMAX. A FAIL is logged as INSPECT.
nomutant   queuing is done by the kernel
test       t/021-queued-usr1.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.022
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With the action SIG_IGN, SIGUSR1 (default action: terminate) sent
           with raise() is discarded: the process goes on and the signal is
           not pending.
test       t/022-sig-ign.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.023
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With SA_RESETHAND and SA_SIGINFO, SA_SIGINFO is clear in the action
           reported by sigaction() inside the handler.
nomutant   the kernel resets the action; a wrapper cannot make it keep
           the flag
test       t/023-resethand-siginfo.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.024
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       With SIGCHLD caught with SA_NOCLDWAIT, after a child exits,
           waitpid() for it (retried after EINTR) fails with ECHILD and the
           process ID names no process (no zombie is left).
note       SI-9 and SI-10: the behaviour is marked XSI in most places;
           the assertion follows that.
test       t/024-nocldwait.c

assertion  XSH.sigaction.025
anchor     functions/sigaction.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       With SIGCHLD caught with SA_NOCLDWAIT and two children, one ending at
           once and one after 300 ms, wait() (retried after EINTR) fails
           with ECHILD no earlier than 250 ms after they were forked.
note       SI-9 and SI-10: the behaviour is marked XSI in most places;
           the assertion follows that.
test       t/025-nocldwait-blocks.c
